{"step_index":151,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T15:01:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T17:01:09+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T17:01:11+02:00\nThe search for \"\"total domination number\" \"at most the Roman domination number\" OR \"total domination number is at most the Roman domination number\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe relationship between the total domination number, denoted by $\\gamma_t(G)$, and the Roman domination number, denoted by $\\gamma_R(G)$, is a known result in graph theory. Specifically, for any graph $G$ (with no isolated vertices to ensure the total domination number is well-defined), the following inequality holds:\n\n$$\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$$\n\nResearch indicates that the Roman domination number is often greater than or equal to the total domination number[1][2][3]. This inequality is frequently cited in studies exploring bounds for various domination parameters in graphs[3]. \n\nIt is worth noting that while $\\gamma_t(G) \\le \\gamma_R(G)$, these parameters are distinct from others like the weak Roman domination number, which may be incomparable to the total domination number[1].\n\nSources:\n[1] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGmGZERfCehzeCwujKSNu2PaDVumyLTz6cA3sOB9W2-6V_wCd21VL7tNNelNf8q05esQva0vnXqq9TgQN0viLtUvZZXQheUNcx39nAs-CcINcImoH5z57y70a40XbAWKlh51DsIP19kdejTC1HpFWPPrOW076sNEWHsU_GUyPFVNwBQWLhH5Y67cjzVu1c=)\n[2] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGmGZERfCehzeCwujKSNu2PaDVumyLTz6cA3sOB9W2-6V_wCd21VL7tNNelNf8q05esQva0vnXqq9TgQN0viLtUvZZXQheUNcx39nAs-CcINcImoH5z57y70a40XbAWKlh51DsIP19kdejTC1HpFWPPrOW076sNEWHsU_GUyPFVNwBQWLhH5Y67cjzVu1c=)\n[3] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGmGZERfCehzeCwujKSNu2PaDVumyLTz6cA3sOB9W2-6V_wCd21VL7tNNelNf8q05esQva0vnXqq9TgQN0viLtUvZZXQheUNcx39nAs-CcINcImoH5z57y70a40XbAWKlh5